<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Women's Social and Political Union</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1903">1903</a>–<a href="/years/1917">1917</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as WSPU, Suffragettes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Women's Social and Political Union was a militant British organisation campaigning for women's right to vote, founded in Manchester in 1903 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmeline-pankhurst">Emmeline Pankhurst</a> and her daughters. Its members, called suffragettes by the press, took the motto 'Deeds, not words': they heckled ministers, smashed windows and set fires, and many went on hunger strike in prison and were force-fed. It suspended its campaign at the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and in 1917 it became the Women's Party.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/womens-social-and-political-union.jpg" alt="Women's Social and Political Union" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Annie_Kenney_and_Christabel_Pankhurst_REST.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
